    "language_probability": 1,
    "text": "In twenty twenty two, America's Commerce Secretary told Congress something strange. Ukrainian soldiers were finding computer chips from dishwashers and refrigerators inside Russian military equipment. Russia had been cut off from the West's technology, so it was raiding kitchen appliances. That was supposed to be the end of the story. It wasn't. Today, investigators are still pulling Western chips, some made just months earlier, out of the wreckage of Russian drones and missiles. And right now, as Washington fights over who gets Nvidias most powerful AI chips, the same question keeps coming back. How does the most sanctioned country on Earth still get the chips it's banned from buying? The answer runs through a maze of shell companies, airports and backroads, stretching from Texas, to Hong, Kong, to the Russian border.",
